module Core

### Protocol for a type whose lifetime is managed by a `using` scope. `$enter` runs on entry
### (returning the value the scope binds — usually `me`) and `$exit` runs on every exit, so the
### resource or lock it guards is acquired on entry and released on exit and its lifetime is
### lexically visible.
###
### Obeying `Enterable` makes a type **using-required**: it must be opened with `using` and cannot
### be used inline, passed as an ordinary argument, or stored in a free `var`. (The `using`
### mechanism itself works for any type that merely has `$enter`/`$exit` — e.g. `Viewing`/`Modifying`
### stay inline-capable precisely by NOT obeying this protocol. Obeying it is the opt-in to the
### stricter "must scope" rule.)
###
### Failability is covariant: `$enter!` MAY fail, but a concrete `$enter` that never fails — a lock
### guard that blocks, or a no-op — is a valid implementation. A resource whose acquisition can
### error expresses that in a failable factory (`open!`) consumed as the `using` resource; `$enter`
### itself usually just returns `me`.
### :see: {Inspecting}, {Claiming}, {FileHandle}
protocol Enterable
  ### Called on entry to the `using` scope. Returns the value the scope binds (usually `me`).
  ### :returns: The entered value as `?Me`.
  routine Me.$enter!() -> Me

  ### Called on every exit from the `using` scope (normal end and every escape). Infallible —
  ### teardown must always be able to run.
  routine Me.$exit()